The obvious objection: the skill now leans on a hook that lives in this
repo's git dir and nowhere else. Run peff-commit in a repo without a
commit-msg hook and the body width is unenforced, where before there was
at least a post-hoc grep. I think that trade is right — the grep was
advisory anyway, it fired after the object was written, and the honest
fix for a repo that cares about width is to install the hook rather than
to have every skill carry its own checker. But it is a real loss of
coverage outside this repo, not a pure simplification.

I considered keeping step 7 in notes-only form, since notes really do
bypass the hook. The fmt pipeline makes it unnecessary: enforcing the
width is cheaper than detecting a violation and then asking whether to
fix it.

The fmt caveat is verified on this host, not assumed. Both plain fmt and
fmt -s join short lines inside a paragraph and double-space after a
sentence end; both leave blank-line paragraph breaks alone. The risk is
a note where line breaks were deliberate — a short enumeration, say —
coming out as one run-on paragraph. PEFF-STYLE.md bans bullet lists in
notes, so that shape should not arise, but fmt will not warn if it does.

The hook itself lives in the shared git dir, so this worktree inherits
it; that is how the 72-char rejection applied to these commits.
